# ==================================================================================
# TEST ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION
# ==================================================================================
# This file contains example configuration for running integration tests.
# Copy this file to `.env.test` and customize for your local environment.
#
# Quick Setup:
#   1. Copy this file: cp .env.test.example .env.test
#   2. Update DATABASE_USER to match your PostgreSQL username (usually your system username)
#   3. Set DATABASE_PASSWORD if your PostgreSQL requires authentication
#   4. Run tests: npm test path/to/test.integration.test.ts
# ==================================================================================

# ==================================================================================
# DATABASE CONFIGURATION FOR TESTS
# ==================================================================================
# The test suite needs to connect to a PostgreSQL database.
# You can either provide a full DATABASE_URL or individual connection parameters.
# Individual parameters will be used to construct the connection string if DATABASE_URL is not set.
# ==================================================================================

# Option 1: Full Database URL (recommended for simplicity)
# If set, this takes precedence over individual DATABASE_* variables
# Format: postgresql://username:password@host:port/database
# Example: DATABASE_URL=postgresql://williamtower@localhost:5432/pricecompare_test
# DATABASE_URL=

# Option 2: Individual Database Connection Parameters
# These are used to construct the DATABASE_URL if it's not explicitly set

# DATABASE_USER - PostgreSQL role/username
# Default: Your system username (process.env.USER)
# Common values:
#   - macOS/Linux: Your system username (e.g., 'williamtower', 'johndoe')
#   - Windows: Usually 'postgres'
#   - Docker: Usually 'postgres'
# DATABASE_USER=williamtower

# DATABASE_PASSWORD - PostgreSQL password (if required)
# Default: Empty string (no password)
# Leave empty if your PostgreSQL doesn't require a password for local connections
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# DATABASE_HOST - PostgreSQL server host
# Default: localhost
# DATABASE_HOST=localhost

# DATABASE_PORT - PostgreSQL server port
# Default: 5432
# DATABASE_PORT=5432

# DATABASE_NAME - Test database name
# Default: pricecompare_test
# IMPORTANT: This database will have tables TRUNCATED during tests
# Never use your production database name here!
# DATABASE_NAME=pricecompare_test

# ==================================================================================
# SECURITY SECRETS (REQUIRED FOR TESTS)
# ==================================================================================
# These secrets are required but use test values by default.
# The defaults in server/test/setup.ts are sufficient for testing.
# Only override these if you need specific test values.
# ==================================================================================

# SESSION_SECRET - Session encryption (test default: 'test-session-secret-min-32-chars-long')
# SESSION_SECRET=

# CSRF_SECRET - CSRF token generation (test default: 'test-csrf-secret-min-32-chars')
# CSRF_SECRET=

# ENCRYPTION_KEY - PII encryption (test default: 64 'a' characters)
# ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ==================================================================================
# REDIS CONFIGURATION (OPTIONAL FOR TESTS)
# ==================================================================================
# Redis is optional for tests. Tests will use in-memory fallbacks if not configured.
# Only set this if you want to test Redis-specific functionality.
# ==================================================================================

# REDIS_URL - Redis connection URL (optional, defaults to empty/in-memory)
# Format: redis://localhost:6379
# REDIS_URL=

# ==================================================================================
# EMAIL CONFIGURATION (OPTIONAL FOR TESTS)
# ==================================================================================
# Email service is disabled by default in tests.
# The defaults in server/test/setup.ts set these to empty strings.
# ==================================================================================

# SMTP_HOST - SMTP server (disabled by default)
# SMTP_HOST=

# SMTP_USER - SMTP username (disabled by default)
# SMTP_USER=

# SMTP_PASS - SMTP password (disabled by default)
# SMTP_PASS=

# ==================================================================================
# TROUBLESHOOTING
# ==================================================================================
#
# ERROR: "role 'postgres' does not exist"
# SOLUTION: Set DATABASE_USER to your PostgreSQL username
#   - Check your username: whoami (macOS/Linux) or echo %USERNAME% (Windows)
#   - Set in .env.test: DATABASE_USER=your_username
#
# ERROR: "database 'pricecompare_test' does not exist"
# SOLUTION: Create the test database
#   - psql -U your_username -c "CREATE DATABASE pricecompare_test;"
#   - Or use createdb: createdb -U your_username pricecompare_test
#
# ERROR: "password authentication failed"
# SOLUTION: Set DATABASE_PASSWORD if required
#   - DATABASE_PASSWORD=your_password
#   - Or configure PostgreSQL to trust local connections (pg_hba.conf)
#
# ERROR: "Connection refused" or "could not connect to server"
# SOLUTION: Check PostgreSQL is running
#   - macOS: brew services start postgresql
#   - Linux: sudo systemctl start postgresql
#   - Windows: Check PostgreSQL service in Services app
#   - Docker: docker-compose up -d postgres
#
# ==================================================================================
